            <ArticleTitle>Keeping in check painful synapses in central amygdala.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>757-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Glutamatergic projections from the parabrachial nucleus to the central amygdala are implicated in pain transmission. In this issue of Neuron, Delaney et al. identify a new form of adrenergic modulation at these synapses, demonstrating that noradrenaline-induced suppression of glutamate release is mediated by a decrease in the number of sites of synaptic transmission without changes in probability of release.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
